# A resistance grant survives the hardness bypass, on a MUNDANE weapon (inc-kapn).
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/gear-bypass-survives.keys 5
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. src/Fight.cpp:2090 sets e.ignoreHardness whenever a
# no-save A_DEQU strikes a NON-magical item. Item::Damage must read that as a
# statement about the METAL -- iron does not resist acid -- and not as a
# statement about the caster's spell, so the owner's gear grant is added AFTER
# the bypass has zeroed the material. This session asks one question: does a
# RESISTANCE reach a plain iron maul that the bypass has stripped to zero?
#
# WHY A PLAIN MAUL, WHERE gear-spell-protection.keys HAD TO USE A MAGIC HAMMER.
# Before inc-kapn the grant was added BEFORE the bypass, so hard = 0 threw the
# spell away with the metal. A resistance was therefore unmeasurable on any
# mundane item, and the sibling script bought a magic weapon to keep a hardness
# alive at all. That is the defect this script exists to catch, so it uses the
# ordinary iron maul the check_dequ_* scripts use: 262 hit points, the heaviest
# ordinary weapon in lib/, so twelve retaliations cannot destroy it and leave no
# page to read.
#
# WHY "PROTECTION FROM ACID" AT CASTER LEVEL TEN. The spell (lib/wspells.irh)
# carries EF_PROTECTS_ITEMS on the same clause that grants AD_ACID, so nothing
# here rests on the sibling-clause question gear-spell-protection.keys settles.
# Its pval is 10d1 + (LEVEL_1PER1), so the resistance is 10 plus the caster
# level: 20 at level ten. It is a level 4 spell, so a mage needs level 7 to hold
# a slot for it; ten is taken because the arithmetic below needs more than 18.
#
# WHY A MAGMA CREEPER, AND WHY THE MARGIN IS REAL. Its A_DEQU is 3d6 AD_ACID
# with NO save DC (lib/mon3.irh), so no Reflex roll decides the outcome and
# hardness is the only thing between the acid and the maul. Iron's acid hardness
# is 10, and the bypass zeroes it, so the resistance is the WHOLE of the number
# on the line. 3d6 cannot exceed 18 and 20 > 18, so no roll can hurt the maul:
# the protection is decisive, not lucky. It has 12 HD and resists blunt, so one
# creeper survives twelve blows; frozen monsters still answer a hit.
#
# The oracle is the game's own combat-numbers line in Item::Damage, which prints
# the hardness actually used:
#   "Maul: 3d6 Acid = 11 vs. 20 (iron) [unhurt]"
# 20 is 0 of bypassed iron plus the 20 the spell grants. With the addition moved
# back above the bypass the same line reads "vs. 0 (iron)".

@include chargen-mage.keys

# chargen-mage.keys stops on the arrival room description. Dismiss it, or the
# [w] below is eaten by the description.
ESC

# Wizard switches, counted from the top of the list in src/Tables.cpp:
#   1 Cheat Death, 3 Free Advancement, 6 Ignore Spell Failure,
#   8 Infinite Mana, 11 Freeze Monsters. A frozen monster takes no turn of its
# own but still ANSWERS a blow, which is the whole point of an A_DEQU check.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
SPACE
DOWN*2
SPACE
DOWN*3
SPACE
DOWN*2
SPACE
DOWN*3
SPACE
ESC

# Nine free levels, on the character screen: 'd' opens it and 'G' gains a level.
# The three @while guards answer the three prompts a mage meets on the way up;
# the metamagic one arrives at level five and is easy to forget, and a level-up
# prompt left unanswered swallows every key after it. SPACE, not ESC, leaves the
# character screen (src/Managers.cpp).
d
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
SPACE

# Teach the mage the one spell this fixture needs. Know All Spells is not used:
# it fills the Spell Manager with pages of spells and @choose reads only what is
# on the screen.
w
@choose "Learn Any Spell"
"Protection from Acid"
ENTER

# Cast it on the caster himself. "*" targets self.
m
@choose "Protection from Acid"
*
@while "more --" SPACE

# --- the weapon whose hit points are measured: an ordinary iron maul ---
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*9                 # walk the glyph row to ordinary weapons
@until "Maul" DOWN
DOWN
ENTER
b                       # Weapon Hand
SPACE
@while "two-handed" y
s                       # stow the starting knife the swap pushed into the air
@until "Things in View" ESC

# The character sheet's Resistances page, as evidence that the grant is really
# on the character, at the magnitude the arithmetic needs.
d
@until "Resistances and Armour" DOWN
@dump:bypass-sheet
@until "Things in View" ESC

@until "SPACE:Swap" i
@until ">>b)Weapon" b
@until "base value of" x
@dump:bypass-before
@until "Things in View" ESC

# One creeper for all twelve blows. Its body is gargantuan, so the summon
# cursor's square is not where the body's western edge lands; the character
# walks east until he bumps into it, and every RIGHT after that is a blow.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Jellies"
@choose "magma creeper"
RIGHT*4 ENTER

RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-01
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-02
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-03
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-04
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-05
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-06
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-07
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-08
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-09
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-10
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-11
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:bypass-strike-12

@until "SPACE:Swap" i
@until ">>b)Weapon" b
@until "base value of" x
@dump:bypass-after
@until "Things in View" ESC
@quit
